### Letta Code / MemFS

This is the strongest reason **not** to build Moltwork memory.

Current Letta MemFS is explicitly Git-backed. `system/` files are always included; other memory is progressively disclosed. Letta also distinguishes external memory, agent-owned skills, message recall and dreaming. ([GitHub][3])

[https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-code](https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-code?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

[https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-docs-md/blob/main/concepts/memfs/index.md](https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-docs-md/blob/main/concepts/memfs/index.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This maps almost perfectly onto Moltwork:

```text
system/
    identity.md
    operating-policy.md

research/
failures/
decisions/
skills/
```

Do **not** copy MemFS into a Moltwork DB.

Record its Git commit/tree digest in `WorkerManifest`.

---

### Agent Skills standard

This has now become a real interoperability primitive rather than merely a Claude convention.

A skill is essentially:

```text
skill/
  SKILL.md
  scripts/
  references/
  assets/
```

with progressive disclosure: metadata is cheap to index; full instructions load on activation; supporting resources load on demand. ([Agent Skills][4])

[https://agentskills.io/specification](https://agentskills.io/specification?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

[https://github.com/anthropics/skills](https://github.com/anthropics/skills?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

[https://github.com/letta-ai/skills](https://github.com/letta-ai/skills?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**This should be the canonical Moltwork process-asset format.**

Don't create `moltwork-skill.json`.

A promoted procedural asset should simply be an Agent Skill plus Moltwork provenance:

```text
skills/hackathon-requirements/
    SKILL.md
    moltwork.provenance.json
```

