# 2. Why Git Is the Memory Solution / Rekal

This is probably the **most directly useful retrieval paper** for Moltwork.

Its important finding isn't merely “store things in Git.” It separates memory questions into different retrieval substrates:

```text
STRUCTURE question
→ repository structural map

POINTED historical question
→ confidence-gated episodes

WHY question
→ decision synthesis
```

The paper reports ~0.31 pooled MRR for its retrieval configuration and argues that dumping episodes indiscriminately can actually degrade answers. Routed answers can be much smaller than the underlying recorded history. ([arXiv][6])

Paper:

[https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14390](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14390?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Implementation:

[https://github.com/rekal-dev/rekal-cli](https://github.com/rekal-dev/rekal-cli?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### Steal this for `moltwork_lab_context`

This is much better than:

```text
semantic search everything
→ shove top 20 chunks into prompt
```

Use a router:

```text
"What modules exist?"
→ inventory / Git tree

"Have we seen this API error?"
→ trajectory episodes

"Why did we reject approach X?"
→ decision synthesis

"What actually happened economically?"
→ WorkerKit ledger

"What procedure do I know?"
→ SKILL.md
```

This is an important architectural primitive.

