# Procedural learning frontier

This is where Moltwork should be especially disciplined: **read and steal algorithms; don't turn them into another runtime.**

## MemP — absolutely relevant

Paper:

[https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06433](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06433?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Code:

[https://github.com/zjunlp/MemP](https://github.com/zjunlp/MemP?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

MemP formalizes procedural memory around:

```text
BUILD
RETRIEVE
UPDATE
```

and distills trajectories into both step-by-step instructions and higher-level scripts, with correction/deprecation as experience accumulates. ([arXiv][9])

### Moltwork mapping

```text
BUILD
→ trajectories + reviews → SkillCandidate

RETRIEVE
→ Letta Agent Skills discovery

UPDATE
→ cg experiment → update/extend/deprecate
```

Do not run MemP as another memory service.

Implement its lifecycle semantics around Agent Skills.

---

# Trace2Skill — probably even more important for your Lab

This appeared in March 2026 and is almost exactly your eventual `Lab → asset` pipeline.

Rather than updating a skill after every individual trace, Trace2Skill analyzes a **pool of trajectories in parallel**, produces trajectory-local lessons, then hierarchically consolidates them into a unified conflict-free skill directory. The authors report cross-model and OOD transfer of the generated declarative skills. ([arXiv][10])

Paper:

[https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25158](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25158?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Official implementation:

[https://github.com/Qwen-Applications/Trace2Skill](https://github.com/Qwen-Applications/Trace2Skill?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For Moltwork I'd adopt the **algorithmic shape** almost verbatim:

```text
5–20 related WorkerRuns
        ↓
parallel trajectory analysts
        ↓
local lessons
        ↓
dedupe/conflict resolution
        ↓
SKILL.md candidate
        ↓
cg
        ↓
promote
```

That is better than having Letta modify a skill after every run.

